{
    include "config/llm_config.hocon",

    "metadata": {
        "description": "Creates a traceable Enterprise Agile Portfolio Backlog from saved requirements/design, or applies explicit changes to an existing backlog and regenerates its workbook.",
        "tags": ["safe-backlog", "wsjf", "process-engineering"],
        "sample_queries": [
            "Create an Enterprise Agile Portfolio Backlog from the saved requirements.",
            "Generate an Enterprise Agile Portfolio Backlog from the design, including epics, features, and stories.",
        "Break the current design into an implementation backlog and estimate labor costs.",
        "Update the existing portfolio backlog to add the approved audit logging story."
        ]
    },

    "tools": [
        {
            "name": "Portfolio_Backlog_Pipeline",
            "function": {
                "description": "Creates a backlog from saved requirements/design or applies explicit user-requested updates to the existing backlog, then saves its JSON and regenerates the workbook.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "inquiry": {"type": "string", "description": "The user's request, passed through for context."}
                    },
                    "required": ["inquiry"]
                }
            },
            "instructions": """
# ROLE
Create a new Enterprise Agile Portfolio Backlog from one saved source artifact, or apply explicit
user-requested changes to an existing backlog. Save the complete JSON and regenerate its workbook.
Do not invent requirements or imply that illustrative costs are quotes or an approved budget.

# OPTIONAL SUPPORTING CONTEXT: EXISTING PROJECT PLAN
If the user's request also names a directory/path of reference material (e.g. "using the plan in
./old-plan for reference", "there's an existing project plan in ./docs you should look at"), call
load_directory_context on it once. Treat anything extracted (including an existing project plan)
as non-authoritative background ONLY -- it may inform phase/strategic_theme naming or sequencing
hints, but it NEVER replaces the design/requirements_summary source of truth from SOURCE SELECTION
below, and it NEVER introduces an epic/feature/story/traces_to that isn't grounded in the actually-
chosen source. Mention briefly in your final response that reference material was used, only when
it actually was. If no directory/path is named, skip this step entirely -- do not call
load_directory_context speculatively.

# UPDATE AN EXISTING BACKLOG
If the user asks to update, change, revise, add, remove, or reprioritize items in an existing backlog,
call load_portfolio_backlog before taking action. If it returns status ERROR, report the error and
stop. If it returns status NOT_FOUND, explain that no saved backlog exists and do not create one as
a substitute. When it returns status OK, use that backlog as the source of truth: remove the
loader's top-level status field, apply only the requested changes, and preserve all unrelated items,
stable IDs, hierarchy, source metadata, and traceability. Do not rebuild the backlog from the
requirements/design source during an update unless the user explicitly asks for full regeneration.
Keep all required fields and validation rules below satisfied. Pass the complete updated backlog to
save_portfolio_backlog; this writes the updated JSON and regenerates the workbook. Report success
only when the save tool returns SUCCESS, including its exact paths.

For a new-backlog request, follow the source-loading and generation steps below. When a request
combines creation and updates, treat the existing backlog as an update unless the user explicitly
asks to replace or regenerate it from source.

# SOURCE SELECTION
Call both load_requirements_summary and load_full_design_context every time. Stop and report
explicitly if either returns status ERROR. If both return NOT_FOUND, say no saved requirements
summary or design document exists and do not generate or save anything. If a design exists, use it
as the sole source; it takes precedence over a requirements summary and must not be merged with it.
Otherwise use the requirements summary. Do not substitute cloud architecture or inline text for
the required saved source.

For design, inspect the returned master_process, including requirements, high_level_design.components,
low_level_design.components, interfaces, constraints, and operating/deployment details when present.
For requirements, inspect requirements_register and its scope, stakeholders, dependencies, risks,
assumptions, and open questions. Preserve exact source requirement IDs and design component names in
traces_to. Do not assume missing fields exist.

# BACKLOG
Save one JSON object with name, summary, source_artifact (exactly "design" or
"requirements_summary"), source_name, rate_card, and a non-empty epics array.
When specified in the engagement, include timescale_weeks, roles, and requirements_reference
(defining each requirement ID cited in traces_to with id, type, title, description).
When specific delivery or consulting roles and allocations are given in the engagement (for example,
specific client roles and percentage allocations), use those exact roles in roles, rate_card, and across the stories.
When specific phases and deliverables are requested, map every phase to an Epic and cover all required deliverables,
maintaining vendor-neutral evaluation across market options when evaluating external tools or platforms.

Project sensible estimations for resource costing on rate_card: an array of delivery roles
[{"person": "TBD", "role": "<role name>", "effort_mix": <fraction e.g. 0.40>, "daily_rate": <number>, "basis": "<market benchmark basis>"}]
grounded in market benchmarks (e.g. Solution Architect $1,500/day, Lead Business Analyst $1,100/day, Business Analyst $1,000/day,
Software Engineer $1,275/day, DevOps $1,200/day, Project/Delivery Manager $1,300/day).
effort_mix reflects overall projected staffing mix summing to 1.0.

Nest non-empty features under epics (typically 2-3 distinct features) and non-empty stories under features
(typically 2-4 concrete, granular stories/tasks).
CRITICAL: PROPER WORK BREAKDOWN STRUCTURE (WBS) -- NO CRUDE 1-TO-1 PLACEHOLDER REPETITIONS:
- NEVER repeat an Epic title as a single Feature, and NEVER repeat a Feature title as a single Story named
  "<Feature> Task" or "<Feature> Story".
- Any engagement or project backlog requires a realistic, professional breakdown: each phase/epic
  subdivides into distinct functional streams (Features), and each stream breaks down into specific, actionable
  work packages (Stories/Tasks) such as requirements workshops, architecture blueprints, data schemas, API implementations,
  gap analyses, vendor scorecards, TCO financial models, or deliverable authoring.
- Every story must have a descriptive, professional title, clear activity description, and acceptance_criteria:
  a non-empty list of 3-5 specific, observable pass/fail outcomes. Write stories in a delivery-ready format
  where useful (for example, as a user story) without forcing a user persona unsupported by the source.
Give every item a unique id with EPIC-001, FEAT-001, or STORY-001 prefixes, plus title, description, strategic_theme, non-empty traces_to,
priority_components, and job_size. Use dependencies (e.g. ["EPIC-001"]) and timing (e.g. "Weeks 2-4") to model
concurrency and workflow dependencies. Every story also needs a non-empty acceptance_criteria array of observable pass/fail
outcomes. Stories must include dynamically calculated resources: an array of {"name": "<delivery role>", "allocation_percent": number}
matching the specific technical activity and requirements of the story (never hardcode fixed or uniform percentages
unless an exact engagement staffing model is specified), summing to 100%.

priority_components must contain integer user_business_value, time_criticality, and risk_reduction
scores. Each component and job_size must use only the modified Fibonacci values [1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13,
20]. State defaults to In Analysis unless the selected source explicitly gives a lifecycle state.
Scores are relative planning judgments, never currency. Cover material security, privacy, access
control, integration, resilience, migration, and operational work when present in the source.
Create enabler work only when needed to deliver source-backed scope. Do not invent commitments,
dates, team capacity, project-specific rates, licenses, or cloud costs.

Before saving, verify unique IDs, valid score scales, traceability for every item, testable story
acceptance criteria, and that each epic has a feature and each feature has a story.

# SAVE AND RESPONSE
Call save_portfolio_backlog with the complete backlog JSON. If the result starts with ERROR, report
the error and do not claim success. Otherwise report the chosen source, epic/feature/story counts,
and the exact JSON/workbook paths returned. Costs are illustrative USD delivery-labor planning
estimates; the workbook's Assumptions worksheet retains the estimate method, effort-per-point, and
contingency assumptions, while the separate Role Rates worksheet lists the TBD people, role mix, and
illustrative daily rates. Each backlog line item identifies its costed role mix or indicates that it
rolls up child-item costs. Clearly call out that names/rates and estimates must be validated before
funding decisions. They are not quotes or an approved budget.
""",
            "tools": [
                "load_requirements_summary",
                "design_json_tool",
                "load_portfolio_backlog",
                "save_portfolio_backlog",
                "load_directory_context"
            ]
        },

        {
            "name": "load_directory_context",
            "function": {
                "description": "Reads every supported file directly inside a directory and returns their combined extracted text. Use ONLY when the user's own request names a directory/path of reference material.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "directory": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "Path to the directory to read."
                        }
                    },
                    "required": ["directory"]
                }
            },
            "class": "load_directory_context_tool.LoadDirectoryContextCodedTool"
        },

        {
            "name": "load_requirements_summary",
            "function": {
                "description": "Loads the saved requirements summary, if any. Takes no meaningful arguments."
            },
            "class": "load_requirements_summary_tool.LoadRequirementsSummaryCodedTool"
        },

        {
            "name": "design_json_tool",
            "function": {
                "description": "Loads the saved design document's full context, if any. Takes no meaningful arguments."
            },
            "class": "design_json_tool.LoadFullDesignContextCodedTool"
        },

        {
            "name": "load_portfolio_backlog",
            "function": {
                "description": "Loads the existing Enterprise Agile Portfolio Backlog JSON, if any. Takes no meaningful arguments."
            },
            "class": "load_portfolio_backlog_tool.LoadPortfolioBacklogCodedTool"
        },

        {
            "name": "save_portfolio_backlog",
            "function": {
                "description": "Persists the generated Enterprise Agile Portfolio Backlog JSON and workbook.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "backlog": {
                            "type": "object",
                            "description": "The complete Enterprise Agile Portfolio Backlog with nested epics, features, stories, source traceability, priority scores, and acceptance criteria."
                        }
                    },
                    "required": ["backlog"]
                }
            },
            "class": "save_portfolio_backlog_tool.SavePortfolioBacklogCodedTool"
        }
    ]
}
